  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n\nSurface high pressure shifts east this weekend which will allow \nwarmer temperatures and higher dew points to move back into the area \nfrom the south. A large mid level ridge builds over the Central US\nwith a northwesterly flow persisting thru the Great Lakes and Ohio\nRiver Valley. This will be a favorable setup for ridge riders \nas shower and thunderstorm chances increase early this week, \n\nRain chances remain low during the day on Sunday with lack of \nforcing before increased rain chances develop Sunday night and into \nearly next week. A signal continues in the model solutions depicting \na potential period for increased storm activity Monday evening and \nMonday night. Moderate instability may end up overlapping with \nstrong shear for late July. Potential exists for MCS development\nacross the Great Lakes tracking into the Ohio Valley from the \nnorthwest. Details on exact timing and placement are uncertain at\nthis time without CAM guidance to resolve potential convection.\n\nAdditionally, storm chances on Sunday night cannot be ruled out with\nfavorable shear and instability in place and some guidance showing \nat least some storm potential in the region. The best chance looks \nto be north of I-70.\n\nAlso, the heat index may reach 95 to 100 degrees on Monday \nafternoon.",
